---
import DocsLayout from "../../../components/DocsLayout.astro";
import CodeBlock from "../../../components/CodeBlock.astro";

const snapshot = `import { useSyncExternalStore } from "react";
import { mdbase } from "./mdbase";

export function useMdbase() {
  return useSyncExternalStore(mdbase.subscribe, mdbase.getSnapshot, mdbase.getSnapshot);
}`;

const phase = `import { useEffect } from "react";
import { mdbase } from "./mdbase";
import { useMdbase } from "./use-mdbase";

export function App() {
  const { phase } = useMdbase();

  useEffect(() => {
    void mdbase.start();
  }, []);

  switch (phase.kind) {
    case "ready":
    case "unsynced":
      return <Worklog key={phase.connection.id} db={phase.connection.records} />;
    case "signed_out":
      return <button onClick={() => mdbase.signIn()}>Sign in with mdbase</button>;
    default:
      return <Waiting phase={phase} />;
  }
}`;

const live = `import type { MdbaseClient, Query, RecordView } from "@mdbase-dev/sdk";
import { useEffect, useState } from "react";

export function useLiveQuery(db: MdbaseClient, query: Query) {
  const [state, setState] = useState<{ records: readonly RecordView[]; stale: boolean }>({
    records: [],
    stale: true,
  });
  // A query is plain data, so its JSON is a stable dependency.
  const key = JSON.stringify(query);

  useEffect(() => {
    const live = db.live(JSON.parse(key) as Query);
    const stop = live.subscribe((next) => setState({ records: next.records, stale: next.stale }));
    return () => {
      stop();
      live.close();
    };
  }, [db, key]);

  return state;
}`;

const use = `function Entries({ db }: { db: MdbaseClient }) {
  const { records, stale } = useLiveQuery(db, {
    types: ["worklog-entry"],
    order_by: [{ field: "date", direction: "desc" }],
    limit: 100,
  });

  return (
    <ul aria-busy={stale}>
      {records.map((entry) => (
        <li key={entry.id}>{String(entry.frontmatter.get("summary"))}</li>
      ))}
    </ul>
  );
}`;

const write = `async function complete(task: RecordView) {
  const write = await db.update(task, { patch: { status: "done" } });
  if (write.state === "rejected") {
    setProblem(describeError(write.receipt.problem)?.message ?? null);
  }
}`;

const status = `import { useMdbase } from "./use-mdbase";

export function SyncStatus() {
  const { sync } = useMdbase();
  return sync ? <span>{sync.text}</span> : null;
}`;
---

<DocsLayout
  title="React"
  description="Use the SDK from React: the snapshot as a store, a hook for live queries, and where the shared components fit."
  active="react"
  headings={[
    { href: "#packages", label: "What there is" },
    { href: "#snapshot", label: "The snapshot as a store" },
    { href: "#live", label: "A live query hook" },
    { href: "#writes", label: "Writes" },
    { href: "#status", label: "Sync status" },
    { href: "#rules", label: "Things to get right" }
  ]}
>
  <h2 id="packages">What there is</h2>
  <p>
    The SDK has no React dependency and needs no provider. Two things make
    it fit React directly:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      The entry from <code>createMdbase()</code> is an external store:
      {" "}<code>subscribe</code> and <code>getSnapshot</code> have the shapes
      {" "}<code>useSyncExternalStore</code> expects.
    </li>
    <li>
      <a href="/sdk/ui/"><code>@mdbase-dev/ui</code></a> has the sign-in and
      collection screens as components. With <code>CollectionGate</code> you
      may not need the first hook on this page at all.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    The <a href="/sdk/connect-quickstart/">Worklog tutorial</a> is a complete
    React app built this way.
  </p>

  <h2 id="snapshot">The snapshot as a store</h2>
  <CodeBlock code={snapshot} language="ts" label="use-mdbase.ts" />
  <p>
    The snapshot is replaced only when something changes, so components
    re-render only then. The third argument is the server snapshot: where the
    SDK cannot run, the phase is <code>unavailable</code>, so a
    server-rendered page gets a defined state.
  </p>
  <CodeBlock code={phase} language="tsx" label="App.tsx" />
  <p>
    Create the entry once, in a module, as the tutorial’s
    {" "}<code>mdbase.ts</code> does. Do not create it inside a component.
  </p>

  <h2 id="live">A live query hook</h2>
  <CodeBlock code={live} language="ts" label="use-live-query.ts" />
  <CodeBlock code={use} language="tsx" label="Entries.tsx" />
  <ul>
    <li>
      The effect’s cleanup both unsubscribes and closes the query. Closing
      tells the collection to stop sending changes.
    </li>
    <li>
      <code>stale</code> is true until the first result arrives, and again
      while a query is reset. The previous records stay in place meanwhile, so
      the list does not flash empty.
    </li>
    <li>
      Give lists a <code>limit</code> and leave bodies out. To page, raise
      the limit; <code>live.setQuery()</code> changes a running query without
      a new subscription.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="writes">Writes</h2>
  <p>
    Call the client from event handlers. A write resolves when it is saved on
    the device, and a live query already shows it, so there is no cache to
    update and nothing to refetch.
  </p>
  <CodeBlock code={write} language="tsx" label="Tasks.tsx" />
  <p>
    For a screen where someone types into one record, use a
    {" "}<a href="/sdk/editing/">record session</a>. It keeps the text field
    and the record in step while other devices edit the same record.
  </p>

  <h2 id="status">Sync status</h2>
  <CodeBlock code={status} language="tsx" label="SyncStatus.tsx" />
  <p>
    <code>snapshot.sync</code> is the open collection’s status as a short
    line of text with counts, and <code>snapshot.writes</code> lists writes
    that are still waiting or were refused. See
    {" "}<a href="/sdk/offline-sync/">Pending writes and sync status</a>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="rules">Things to get right</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Key your screen on the connection.</strong>
      {" "}<code>key=&#123;connection.id&#125;</code> resets component state
      when the user switches collection.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Call sign-in from the click.</strong>
      {" "}<code>onClick=&#123;() =&gt; mdbase.signIn()&#125;</code> works.
      Awaiting something first loses the browser’s permission to open the
      window.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Strict Mode is fine.</strong> <code>start()</code> is safe to
      call twice, and the live query hook above cleans up after the first
      mount.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Server rendering.</strong> The SDK runs in the browser.
      Creating the entry and reading its snapshot on the server is safe; the
      phase there is <code>unavailable</code>. Call <code>start()</code> only
      in the browser, from an effect: on the server it rejects.
    </li>
  </ul>
</DocsLayout>
